      "paperId": "b19bc412358351e5cf5eb9159d4d2246106a2875",
      "title": "Sounding Allegory, Figuring the Word: Sound and Image Dissonance in Walter Benjamin\u2019s Origin of the German Trauerspiel",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Hong Liu",
          "authorId": "2364183239"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "In the Origin of the German\u00a0Trauerspiel, Walter Benjamin recuperates allegory as a mode of expression that resists hegemonic meaning and symbolic identity\u2014one that signals instead a non-identitarian, fragmented, and heterogeneous relation to knowledge and history instead. While Benjamin\u2019s allegory has been received as a pre-eminently visual mode, Trauerspiel\u2019s story of image is related to another story of sound. By beginning with the reference to Jonathan Sterne\u2019s \u2018audiovisual litany,\u2019 this paper aims to\u00a0interrupt the by now customary attention to Benjamin\u2019s images, and to rediscover their sonorous accompaniment. The paper revisits the Trauerspielbuch\u2019s central claim about allegory, namely that baroque allegory is philosophically grounded in the relationship between\u00a0language and script, or more precisely, in the unfulfilled unity between sound and image. Focusing on the discussion of speech sound and image-script (Schriftbild) in Origin, this paper discusses how Benjamin contextualized Trauerspiel in early modern print culture and linguistic theory, claiming it as a form that is both mute and sonorous. Further, in the dissonance of image and sound, mourning emerges from the search for an absent\u00a0voice.\u00a0Complicating the\u00a0traditional critique of allegory as a mute form that\u00a0imprisons spirit, the paper traces its reconfiguration through\u00a0the alterity of the Baroque Schriftbild, the romantic allegorization of sound, and the musical dimension of the Trauerspiel. In doing so, it explores the anti-phonocentric thrust of Benjamin\u2019s valorization of script and reconsiders lament as, to borrow Mladen Dolar\u2019s term, acousmatic \u2018object voice,\u2019 in order to propose new ways of envisioning the aesthetic and ethical potential of Benjamin\u2019s allegorical Trauerspiel.",
